# False-positive validation of the census inconsistency flags

_Generated 2026-06-25 by `adjudicate_inconsistencies.py` over the 5,053-paper census._

Flagged inconsistent claims (raw): **219**  (decision-changing: 74)

## Breakdown (transparent rules)

| category | n | of which decision-changing |
|---|---|---|
| TRUE_LIKELY | 102 | 16 |
| REVIEW_P_BOUND | 9 | 3 |
| FP_ONE_TAILED | 28 | 6 |
| FP_MISEXTRACTION | 80 | 49 |

## Corrected reading
- **clear false positives: 108/219 = 49%** (80 mis-extraction + 28 one-tailed)
- ambiguous (p reported as a bound), needs review: 9
- **likely-true internal inconsistencies: 102/219 = 47%**

So the raw ~15% claim-inconsistency rate OVERSTATES real errors: a large share are extractor p-mis-pairing (the claim's own text has no p) or one-sided p-values. The defensible **true** rate is the TRUE_LIKELY fraction (a LOWER bound; REVIEW_P_BOUND may add some). **Biggest actionable bug: the extractor attaches a p-value from a neighbouring claim when the statistic's own text has none → fix the p-attachment proximity/scope before the formal census.**

