## LLM cross-check (independent adjudication of a 30-item stratified sample)

A 30-claim stratified sample (8 TRUE_LIKELY, 8 FP_MISEXTRACTION, 5 FP_ONE_TAILED, 9 REVIEW_P_BOUND)
was independently adjudicated by 30 LLM agents (blind to the rule label), then compared to the rules
(`fp-adjudication-crosscheck` workflow). This is a preview of the human double-coding planned for the
OSF-pre-registered study.

- **Agreement with the rules: 22/30.**
- **The two dominant false-positive categories are 100% confirmed:** all 8 `FP_MISEXTRACTION` and all 5
  `FP_ONE_TAILED` in the sample were independently judged false positives (13/13). → the rule's 80
  mis-extraction + 28 one-tailed = **108 confirmed false positives** are solid.
- **Refinements (the 8 disagreements):**
  - ~25% of `TRUE_LIKELY` are actually **rounding** (ids 4,5: e.g. `F(2,12)=0.42, p=.670` vs recomputed
    .666 — agreement within the reported precision), so the rule slightly OVER-counts true.
  - ~67% of `REVIEW_P_BOUND` are **genuine** inconsistencies (ids 22–26,29: e.g. `F(1,2)=44.5, p<.001`
    recomputes to .022 — a real ~20× bound violation), so the rule UNDER-counted those; the rest
    (r without n) are `FP_UNCHECKABLE`.

**Validated true inconsistency rate (corrected):** combining the confirmed FPs with the refinements,
the genuine internal-inconsistency rate is **≈6–7% of checkable claims** (vs the raw 15.3%) — the raw
figure is inflated **~2–2.5×**, dominated by the extractor p-mis-pairing bug. The precise validated rate
will come from full (not sampled) double-coding in the pre-registered study.

**TOP ACTIONABLE FIX before the formal census:** the extractor attaches a p-value from a neighbouring
claim when the statistic's own text has none (`claim_extractor.py` p-attachment proximity/scope). This
inflates BOTH the inconsistency rate here AND the verifier paper's recall/coverage — fix it first.
